One approach is to try to peek at the next byte without consuming it. But Rust's BufRead doesn't expose that easily. Another approach is to read a line and see if there is one, then put it back... but that's awkward.

Actually, looking at the logic - if stdin_available is true and files is empty, I should process it. But if stdin_available is false and files is empty, I should error. Let me check my conditions again:

```rust
if stdin_available && files.is_empty() && !timestamp_input && headless {
